1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,136
Hi, I'm Ryan Rover.

2
00:00:02,316 --> 00:00:03,901
There are two kinds of rich in

3
00:00:03,901 --> 00:00:04,324
America.

4
00:00:04,504 --> 00:00:05,613
There's old money,

5
00:00:05,613 --> 00:00:08,510
families who have been wealthy for generations,

6
00:00:08,510 --> 00:00:10,913
quietly, often without anyone noticing.

7
00:00:11,093 --> 00:00:12,521
And there's new money,

8
00:00:12,521 --> 00:00:15,183
people who made their fortune themselves,

9
00:00:15,183 --> 00:00:17,455
often fast, and often very visibly.

10
00:00:17,635 --> 00:00:18,912
They both have money.

11
00:00:19,092 --> 00:00:20,838
But they tend to think about it,

12
00:00:20,838 --> 00:00:23,075
spend it and protect it very differently.

13
00:00:23,255 --> 00:00:26,250
Today, twenty differences between old money and

14
00:00:26,250 --> 00:00:28,416
new money, and the lessons you can

15
00:00:28,416 --> 00:00:29,436
steal from both,

16
00:00:29,436 --> 00:00:31,858
whatever your bank balance looks like.

17
00:00:32,207 --> 00:00:34,208
Here's a number you'll hear a lot

18
00:00:34,208 --> 00:00:35,481
in wealth management.

19
00:00:35,661 --> 00:00:37,795
A widely cited study by the Williams

20
00:00:37,795 --> 00:00:40,224
Group found that about seventy percent of

21
00:00:40,224 --> 00:00:42,653
wealthy families lose their wealth by the

22
00:00:42,653 --> 00:00:45,379
second generation, and about ninety percent by

23
00:00:45,379 --> 00:00:45,971
the third.

24
00:00:46,321 --> 00:00:47,789
That research is debated,

25
00:00:47,789 --> 00:00:50,431
and different studies measure it differently.

26
00:00:50,611 --> 00:00:52,639
But the pattern is so common it

27
00:00:52,639 --> 00:00:53,816
has an old saying.

28
00:00:53,996 --> 00:00:57,142
Shirtsleeves to shirtsleeves in three generations.

29
00:00:57,322 --> 00:00:59,823
The first generation builds the fortune in

30
00:00:59,823 --> 00:01:00,538
work shirts.

31
00:01:00,718 --> 00:01:02,042
The second enjoys it.

32
00:01:02,222 --> 00:01:04,416
The third is back in shirtsleeves.

33
00:01:04,596 --> 00:01:06,117
Old money families are,

34
00:01:06,117 --> 00:01:08,630
by definition, the ones who broke that

35
00:01:08,630 --> 00:01:09,159
pattern.

36
00:01:09,339 --> 00:01:11,823
That's why their habits are worth studying.

37
00:01:12,174 --> 00:01:14,587
We'll compare old and new money from

38
00:01:14,587 --> 00:01:15,459
three angles.

39
00:01:15,639 --> 00:01:17,102
First, the numbers.

40
00:01:17,282 --> 00:01:19,006
How each group earns,

41
00:01:19,006 --> 00:01:21,880
invests, borrows and handles taxes.

42
00:01:22,060 --> 00:01:23,975
Second, the psychology.

43
00:01:24,155 --> 00:01:26,162
How they think about status,

44
00:01:26,162 --> 00:01:27,882
risk, time and identity.

45
00:01:28,062 --> 00:01:29,664
And third, the playbook.

46
00:01:29,844 --> 00:01:32,579
The practical systems old money families use

47
00:01:32,579 --> 00:01:34,505
to keep wealth for generations,

48
00:01:34,505 --> 00:01:36,929
and the strengths new money brings that

49
00:01:36,929 --> 00:01:38,296
old money often lacks.

50
00:01:38,476 --> 00:01:39,568
I want to be clear.

51
00:01:39,748 --> 00:01:41,356
These are broad patterns,

52
00:01:41,356 --> 00:01:42,000
not rules.

53
00:01:42,180 --> 00:01:45,315
Plenty of individuals break the stereotypes in

54
00:01:45,315 --> 00:01:46,406
both directions.

55
00:01:46,756 --> 00:01:48,358
Let's start with the numbers.

56
00:01:48,538 --> 00:01:50,218
And the first difference is about where

57
00:01:50,218 --> 00:01:51,510
the money actually comes from.

58
00:01:51,859 --> 00:01:52,881
Our sources.

59
00:01:53,061 --> 00:01:55,800
Family wealth transfer research comes from the

60
00:01:55,800 --> 00:01:58,599
Williams Group, published in the book Preparing

61
00:01:58,599 --> 00:02:00,921
Heirs by Roy Williams and Vic Preisser.

62
00:02:01,101 --> 00:02:04,872
Billionaire data comes from UBS's Billionaire Ambitions

63
00:02:04,872 --> 00:02:05,420
reports.

64
00:02:05,600 --> 00:02:09,243
Millionaire profiles come from Ramsey Solutions' National

65
00:02:09,243 --> 00:02:12,566
Study of Millionaires and Thomas Stanley's research.

66
00:02:12,746 --> 00:02:15,147
The Vanderbilt story comes from the book

67
00:02:15,147 --> 00:02:17,007
Fortune's Children by Arthur T.

68
00:02:17,187 --> 00:02:18,429
Vanderbilt the second.

69
00:02:18,609 --> 00:02:20,653
Tax rules come from the IRS.

70
00:02:20,833 --> 00:02:22,208
This is education,

71
00:02:22,208 --> 00:02:23,813
not financial advice,

72
00:02:23,813 --> 00:02:25,953
and not tax or legal advice.

73
00:02:26,302 --> 00:02:27,080
Angle one.

74
00:02:27,260 --> 00:02:28,027
The numbers.

75
00:02:28,207 --> 00:02:31,543
Seven financial differences between old money and

76
00:02:31,543 --> 00:02:32,224
new money.

77
00:02:32,574 --> 00:02:33,885
Difference number one.

78
00:02:34,065 --> 00:02:36,074
Old money lives on assets.

79
00:02:36,254 --> 00:02:38,193
New money often lives on income.

80
00:02:38,542 --> 00:02:41,077
Many new money fortunes come from a

81
00:02:41,077 --> 00:02:43,539
high income or a single big event,

82
00:02:43,539 --> 00:02:45,205
like selling a company,

83
00:02:45,205 --> 00:02:47,884
a big contract or a successful career

84
00:02:47,884 --> 00:02:49,839
in sports or entertainment.

85
00:02:50,019 --> 00:02:51,655
That money arrives as cash,

86
00:02:51,655 --> 00:02:53,049
and it's easy to spend.

87
00:02:53,229 --> 00:02:54,485
Old money families,

88
00:02:54,485 --> 00:02:57,195
by contrast, typically live on what their

89
00:02:57,195 --> 00:02:58,187
assets produce.

90
00:02:58,367 --> 00:03:02,105
Rents, dividends, interest and business profits.

91
00:03:02,285 --> 00:03:04,455
The core assets stay untouched,

92
00:03:04,455 --> 00:03:06,555
and only part of the income is

93
00:03:06,555 --> 00:03:06,976
spent.

94
00:03:07,325 --> 00:03:08,823
That's a crucial difference.

95
00:03:09,003 --> 00:03:10,834
If you spend from income,

96
00:03:10,834 --> 00:03:12,592
a job loss or a bad year

97
00:03:12,592 --> 00:03:13,984
can end everything.

98
00:03:14,164 --> 00:03:15,814
If you spend only part of what

99
00:03:15,814 --> 00:03:16,914
your assets produce,

100
00:03:16,914 --> 00:03:19,004
the wealth can keep growing even while

101
00:03:19,004 --> 00:03:19,830
you live on it.

102
00:03:20,010 --> 00:03:22,582
Many old money families follow a version

103
00:03:22,582 --> 00:03:24,189
of this rule for decades.

104
00:03:24,369 --> 00:03:25,997
Spend some of the returns,

105
00:03:25,997 --> 00:03:27,248
never the principal.

106
00:03:27,599 --> 00:03:28,910
The lesson for anyone.

107
00:03:29,090 --> 00:03:32,256
Start building assets that produce income,

108
00:03:32,256 --> 00:03:33,537
like index funds,

109
00:03:33,537 --> 00:03:35,120
a retirement account,

110
00:03:35,120 --> 00:03:37,984
a rental property or a small business.

111
00:03:38,164 --> 00:03:39,862
Over time, aim for a day when

112
00:03:39,862 --> 00:03:42,028
part of your living costs are covered

113
00:03:42,028 --> 00:03:43,491
by what your assets earn,

114
00:03:43,491 --> 00:03:45,014
not just by what you earn.

115
00:03:45,363 --> 00:03:47,384
Here's a simple way to picture it.

116
00:03:47,564 --> 00:03:49,927
Imagine two people who each have two

117
00:03:49,927 --> 00:03:50,977
million dollars.

118
00:03:51,157 --> 00:03:52,963
One spends it like income,

119
00:03:52,963 --> 00:03:55,604
buying a bigger house and better cars.

120
00:03:55,784 --> 00:03:58,011
The other invests it and spends only

121
00:03:58,011 --> 00:03:59,867
a small share of what it earns

122
00:03:59,867 --> 00:04:00,486
each year.

123
00:04:00,666 --> 00:04:01,752
Twenty years later,

124
00:04:01,752 --> 00:04:03,868
the first person may have very little

125
00:04:03,868 --> 00:04:06,041
left, while the second person may have

126
00:04:06,041 --> 00:04:07,643
more than they started with,

127
00:04:07,643 --> 00:04:09,301
and has been living on it the

128
00:04:09,301 --> 00:04:09,930
whole time.

129
00:04:10,280 --> 00:04:11,534
Difference number two.

130
00:04:11,714 --> 00:04:14,118
Old money thinks in generations.

131
00:04:14,298 --> 00:04:16,271
New money often thinks in years.

132
00:04:16,621 --> 00:04:18,610
When you've just made your fortune,

133
00:04:18,610 --> 00:04:20,883
the natural questions are about the next

134
00:04:20,883 --> 00:04:21,451
few years.

135
00:04:21,631 --> 00:04:22,525
What should I buy?

136
00:04:22,705 --> 00:04:23,971
How do I enjoy this?

137
00:04:24,151 --> 00:04:27,371
Old money families often frame decisions around

138
00:04:27,371 --> 00:04:29,015
decades and generations.

139
00:04:29,195 --> 00:04:31,622
Will this still matter for our grandchildren?

140
00:04:31,802 --> 00:04:33,909
That longer view changes everything,

141
00:04:33,909 --> 00:04:35,781
from how they invest to how they

142
00:04:35,781 --> 00:04:36,132
spend.

143
00:04:36,482 --> 00:04:39,317
A long time horizon makes compounding work

144
00:04:39,317 --> 00:04:40,465
at full strength.

145
00:04:40,645 --> 00:04:42,937
Money invested for fifty years,

146
00:04:42,937 --> 00:04:44,860
or for multiple lifetimes,

147
00:04:44,860 --> 00:04:46,339
can grow enormously,

148
00:04:46,339 --> 00:04:48,040
even at modest returns.

149
00:04:48,220 --> 00:04:50,674
It also makes families more patient with

150
00:04:50,674 --> 00:04:52,822
market drops, because a bad year is

151
00:04:52,822 --> 00:04:54,601
a tiny part of a hundred year

152
00:04:54,601 --> 00:04:54,908
plan.

153
00:04:55,258 --> 00:04:56,697
The lesson for anyone.

154
00:04:56,877 --> 00:04:58,514
Stretch your time horizon.

155
00:04:58,694 --> 00:04:59,973
When you invest,

156
00:04:59,973 --> 00:05:01,333
think in decades,

157
00:05:01,333 --> 00:05:02,212
not months.

158
00:05:02,392 --> 00:05:04,589
Ask yourself what decision your seventy year

159
00:05:04,589 --> 00:05:06,038
old self would thank you for.

160
00:05:06,388 --> 00:05:08,889
Endowments at some of the oldest American

161
00:05:08,889 --> 00:05:11,452
universities show what a very long horizon

162
00:05:11,452 --> 00:05:11,879
can do.

163
00:05:12,059 --> 00:05:14,185
Funds that spend only a small share

164
00:05:14,185 --> 00:05:16,189
each year, and reinvest the rest,

165
00:05:16,189 --> 00:05:18,618
have grown over generations into some of

166
00:05:18,618 --> 00:05:20,623
the largest pools of money in the

167
00:05:20,623 --> 00:05:20,987
world.

168
00:05:21,167 --> 00:05:23,408
The principle works at any scale.

169
00:05:23,588 --> 00:05:26,040
Patience plus a modest spending rate lets

170
00:05:26,040 --> 00:05:28,313
money outlive the people who saved it.

171
00:05:28,663 --> 00:05:29,916
Difference number three.

172
00:05:30,096 --> 00:05:31,861
Old money spreads its bets.

173
00:05:32,041 --> 00:05:34,212
New money is often concentrated.

174
00:05:34,563 --> 00:05:37,790
New fortunes are frequently built by concentration.

175
00:05:37,970 --> 00:05:40,018
A founder owns a huge stake in

176
00:05:40,018 --> 00:05:40,838
one company.

177
00:05:41,018 --> 00:05:43,544
An athlete has one skill that pays

178
00:05:43,544 --> 00:05:44,361
enormously.

179
00:05:44,541 --> 00:05:47,421
Concentration is how many fortunes are made.

180
00:05:47,601 --> 00:05:49,703
But it's also how many are lost,

181
00:05:49,703 --> 00:05:52,396
when a single company or career declines.

182
00:05:52,746 --> 00:05:55,904
Old money families tend to diversify across

183
00:05:55,904 --> 00:05:56,786
many assets.

184
00:05:56,966 --> 00:05:59,462
Stocks, bonds, real estate,

185
00:05:59,462 --> 00:06:02,051
private businesses and more.

186
00:06:02,231 --> 00:06:05,017
They accept that diversification may mean they

187
00:06:05,017 --> 00:06:07,137
won't get the very highest returns,

188
00:06:07,137 --> 00:06:09,681
in exchange for protecting the wealth from

189
00:06:09,681 --> 00:06:10,892
any single disaster.

190
00:06:11,072 --> 00:06:12,976
Their goal is not to get rich.

191
00:06:13,156 --> 00:06:14,317
It's to stay rich.

192
00:06:14,497 --> 00:06:17,297
As one saying in wealth management puts

193
00:06:17,297 --> 00:06:19,595
it, concentration builds wealth,

194
00:06:19,595 --> 00:06:21,893
and diversification protects it.

195
00:06:22,243 --> 00:06:23,543
The lesson for anyone.

196
00:06:23,723 --> 00:06:25,917
Concentration can build wealth,

197
00:06:25,917 --> 00:06:28,252
for example in your own career or

198
00:06:28,252 --> 00:06:28,889
business.

199
00:06:29,069 --> 00:06:30,722
But once you have savings,

200
00:06:30,722 --> 00:06:31,740
spread them out.

201
00:06:31,920 --> 00:06:34,160
Broad low cost index funds are a

202
00:06:34,160 --> 00:06:36,959
simple way for ordinary investors to own

203
00:06:36,959 --> 00:06:39,129
small pieces of many companies.

204
00:06:39,480 --> 00:06:41,839
Think of a founder whose entire net

205
00:06:41,839 --> 00:06:43,996
worth is in one company's stock.

206
00:06:44,176 --> 00:06:45,685
If that company thrives,

207
00:06:45,685 --> 00:06:47,194
they become even richer.

208
00:06:47,374 --> 00:06:48,368
If it collapses,

209
00:06:48,368 --> 00:06:50,542
as many once famous companies have,

210
00:06:50,542 --> 00:06:52,715
their fortune can collapse with it.

211
00:06:52,895 --> 00:06:55,724
Old money families have usually lived through

212
00:06:55,724 --> 00:06:57,988
enough cycles to know that no single

213
00:06:57,988 --> 00:07:00,565
company, industry or country stays on top

214
00:07:00,565 --> 00:07:01,068
forever.

215
00:07:01,419 --> 00:07:02,696
Difference number four.

216
00:07:02,876 --> 00:07:03,979
How they use debt.

217
00:07:04,329 --> 00:07:06,546
Some new money spends on visible things

218
00:07:06,546 --> 00:07:07,684
with borrowed money.

219
00:07:07,864 --> 00:07:09,560
Luxury cars on loans,

220
00:07:09,560 --> 00:07:11,901
huge mortgages on show homes,

221
00:07:11,901 --> 00:07:13,274
big credit lines.

222
00:07:13,454 --> 00:07:14,708
When income drops,

223
00:07:14,708 --> 00:07:16,658
that debt becomes dangerous.

224
00:07:16,838 --> 00:07:19,614
Many famous athletes and entertainers who went

225
00:07:19,614 --> 00:07:21,727
broke had large debts tied to their

226
00:07:21,727 --> 00:07:22,330
lifestyle.

227
00:07:22,680 --> 00:07:23,992
Old money families,

228
00:07:23,992 --> 00:07:25,166
when they borrow,

229
00:07:25,166 --> 00:07:28,066
tend to borrow against assets for specific

230
00:07:28,066 --> 00:07:30,068
purposes, often at low rates,

231
00:07:30,068 --> 00:07:31,933
and rarely for consumption.

232
00:07:32,113 --> 00:07:35,843
Some wealthy families borrow against their investment

233
00:07:35,843 --> 00:07:37,954
portfolios instead of selling,

234
00:07:37,954 --> 00:07:40,066
which can have tax advantages.

235
00:07:40,246 --> 00:07:42,787
But that strategy carries real risks and

236
00:07:42,787 --> 00:07:44,566
only makes sense with large,

237
00:07:44,566 --> 00:07:47,107
diversified assets and careful planning.

238
00:07:47,457 --> 00:07:48,815
The lesson for anyone.

239
00:07:48,995 --> 00:07:51,793
Avoid borrowing for things that lose value.

240
00:07:51,973 --> 00:07:53,115
If you do borrow,

241
00:07:53,115 --> 00:07:55,400
borrow for things that can grow or

242
00:07:55,400 --> 00:07:58,156
produce income, keep the debt manageable,

243
00:07:58,156 --> 00:08:00,239
and know exactly what it costs.

244
00:08:00,590 --> 00:08:02,784
History is full of examples.

245
00:08:02,964 --> 00:08:05,727
Many professional athletes earn more in a

246
00:08:05,727 --> 00:08:08,018
few years than most people earn in

247
00:08:08,018 --> 00:08:10,511
a lifetime, yet a widely cited Sports

248
00:08:10,511 --> 00:08:13,678
Illustrated report in two thousand nine claimed

249
00:08:13,678 --> 00:08:15,834
that a large share of former NFL

250
00:08:15,834 --> 00:08:18,866
and NBA players faced financial stress within

251
00:08:18,866 --> 00:08:20,483
a few years of retiring.

252
00:08:20,663 --> 00:08:22,310
Whatever the exact share,

253
00:08:22,310 --> 00:08:23,891
the pattern is familiar.

254
00:08:24,071 --> 00:08:26,179
Big income, big lifestyle,

255
00:08:26,179 --> 00:08:29,179
big debts, and then the income stops.

256
00:08:29,530 --> 00:08:30,830
Difference number five.

257
00:08:31,010 --> 00:08:33,460
Old money plans for taxes years ahead.

258
00:08:33,810 --> 00:08:35,915
Many people with new money get a

259
00:08:35,915 --> 00:08:37,823
painful surprise at tax time,

260
00:08:37,823 --> 00:08:40,915
especially after selling a company or receiving

261
00:08:40,915 --> 00:08:41,902
a large payout.

262
00:08:42,082 --> 00:08:45,038
Old money families typically work with tax

263
00:08:45,038 --> 00:08:47,501
professionals and plan years ahead,

264
00:08:47,501 --> 00:08:50,879
using legal strategies like retirement accounts,

265
00:08:50,879 --> 00:08:54,187
charitable giving, trusts and careful timing of

266
00:08:54,187 --> 00:08:54,609
sales.

267
00:08:54,959 --> 00:08:57,319
One well known rule is the step

268
00:08:57,319 --> 00:08:58,233
up in basis.

269
00:08:58,413 --> 00:09:00,074
Under current US tax law,

270
00:09:00,074 --> 00:09:03,330
when someone inherits certain assets like stocks,

271
00:09:03,330 --> 00:09:05,722
the cost basis is generally reset to

272
00:09:05,722 --> 00:09:07,782
the value at the time of death.

273
00:09:07,962 --> 00:09:10,835
That can greatly reduce capital gains taxes

274
00:09:10,835 --> 00:09:11,503
for heirs.

275
00:09:11,683 --> 00:09:13,819
Laws change, and details matter,

276
00:09:13,819 --> 00:09:16,756
so families work with professionals on this.

277
00:09:17,107 --> 00:09:18,395
The lesson for anyone.

278
00:09:18,575 --> 00:09:20,411
You don't need a family office to

279
00:09:20,411 --> 00:09:21,246
plan for taxes.

280
00:09:21,426 --> 00:09:24,131
Use tax advantaged retirement accounts,

281
00:09:24,131 --> 00:09:27,391
understand how long you hold investments before

282
00:09:27,391 --> 00:09:29,541
selling, and keep good records.

283
00:09:29,721 --> 00:09:31,404
A little planning can save a lot.

284
00:09:31,755 --> 00:09:33,229
For regular families,

285
00:09:33,229 --> 00:09:35,686
the same mindset shows up in simple

286
00:09:35,686 --> 00:09:36,248
choices.

287
00:09:36,428 --> 00:09:38,697
Contributing to a four oh one k

288
00:09:38,697 --> 00:09:41,185
before taxes, using a Roth IRA for

289
00:09:41,185 --> 00:09:42,357
tax free growth,

290
00:09:42,357 --> 00:09:45,211
holding investments more than a year so

291
00:09:45,211 --> 00:09:47,920
gains may qualify for lower long term

292
00:09:47,920 --> 00:09:49,384
capital gains rates,

293
00:09:49,384 --> 00:09:51,799
and keeping receipts and records.

294
00:09:51,979 --> 00:09:53,447
None of it is glamorous,

295
00:09:53,447 --> 00:09:55,283
but over a lifetime it can add

296
00:09:55,283 --> 00:09:57,057
up to a large amount of money

297
00:09:57,057 --> 00:09:58,341
kept instead of paid.

298
00:09:58,692 --> 00:10:00,154
Difference number six.

299
00:10:00,334 --> 00:10:01,368
How much they spend.

300
00:10:01,718 --> 00:10:03,609
New money can spend at a pace

301
00:10:03,609 --> 00:10:05,827
that outruns even a large fortune.

302
00:10:06,007 --> 00:10:07,583
Big houses, staff,

303
00:10:07,583 --> 00:10:10,909
cars, travel and gifts for friends and

304
00:10:10,909 --> 00:10:11,522
family.

305
00:10:11,702 --> 00:10:13,565
The fortune feels endless,

306
00:10:13,565 --> 00:10:14,640
until it isn't.

307
00:10:14,820 --> 00:10:17,599
Stories of lottery winners and athletes who

308
00:10:17,599 --> 00:10:20,184
went broke are common because big sudden

309
00:10:20,184 --> 00:10:23,028
wealth often comes with big sudden spending.

310
00:10:23,378 --> 00:10:25,822
Many old money families follow a spending

311
00:10:25,822 --> 00:10:26,477
discipline.

312
00:10:26,657 --> 00:10:28,780
Some foundations and endowments,

313
00:10:28,780 --> 00:10:31,234
for example, aim to spend around four

314
00:10:31,234 --> 00:10:33,622
to five percent of their assets each

315
00:10:33,622 --> 00:10:35,878
year, so the principal can keep up

316
00:10:35,878 --> 00:10:37,536
with inflation over time.

317
00:10:37,716 --> 00:10:40,885
Families that last often follow something similar.

318
00:10:41,236 --> 00:10:42,652
The lesson for anyone.

319
00:10:42,832 --> 00:10:44,248
Know your spending rate.

320
00:10:44,428 --> 00:10:45,895
What share of your income do you

321
00:10:45,895 --> 00:10:46,170
spend?

322
00:10:46,350 --> 00:10:48,279
What share of your investments would you

323
00:10:48,279 --> 00:10:49,485
need to withdraw to live?

324
00:10:49,665 --> 00:10:52,499
Keeping that number sustainable is how wealth

325
00:10:52,499 --> 00:10:53,066
survives.

326
00:10:53,417 --> 00:10:54,949
Think about lottery winners.

327
00:10:55,129 --> 00:10:57,338
Many receive a fortune overnight,

328
00:10:57,338 --> 00:10:59,949
with no experience managing large sums,

329
00:10:59,949 --> 00:11:02,828
and with friends and family suddenly asking

330
00:11:02,828 --> 00:11:03,430
for help.

331
00:11:03,610 --> 00:11:06,765
Researchers studying lottery winners in Florida found

332
00:11:06,765 --> 00:11:09,086
that larger winners were less likely to

333
00:11:09,086 --> 00:11:11,348
file for bankruptcy in the short term,

334
00:11:11,348 --> 00:11:13,729
but that over time the protective effect

335
00:11:13,729 --> 00:11:14,860
faded considerably.

336
00:11:15,040 --> 00:11:18,027
Sudden money without a spending discipline is

337
00:11:18,027 --> 00:11:18,558
fragile.

338
00:11:18,908 --> 00:11:20,138
Difference number seven.

339
00:11:20,318 --> 00:11:21,328
How they protect wealth.

340
00:11:21,679 --> 00:11:25,105
Old money families often use legal structures,

341
00:11:25,105 --> 00:11:28,605
like trusts, family partnerships and insurance,

342
00:11:28,605 --> 00:11:30,988
to protect wealth from lawsuits,

343
00:11:30,988 --> 00:11:33,521
divorce, poor decisions and taxes.

344
00:11:33,701 --> 00:11:36,759
These structures can also control how heirs

345
00:11:36,759 --> 00:11:39,818
receive money, for example in stages rather

346
00:11:39,818 --> 00:11:41,027
than all at once.

347
00:11:41,377 --> 00:11:43,536
New money is often more exposed.

348
00:11:43,716 --> 00:11:46,186
Assets may sit in one person's name,

349
00:11:46,186 --> 00:11:48,244
without a will or estate plan.

350
00:11:48,424 --> 00:11:50,881
Surveys have found that many Americans,

351
00:11:50,881 --> 00:11:53,339
including many with significant assets,

352
00:11:53,339 --> 00:11:54,473
don't have a will.

353
00:11:54,653 --> 00:11:57,855
That leaves families vulnerable to court processes

354
00:11:57,855 --> 00:11:58,751
and conflicts.

355
00:11:59,101 --> 00:12:00,413
The lesson for anyone.

356
00:12:00,593 --> 00:12:02,197
At minimum, have a will,

357
00:12:02,197 --> 00:12:04,604
name beneficiaries on every account,

358
00:12:04,604 --> 00:12:06,409
and carry enough insurance,

359
00:12:06,409 --> 00:12:08,348
including liability coverage.

360
00:12:08,528 --> 00:12:11,010
If you have significant assets or young

361
00:12:11,010 --> 00:12:13,938
children, talk to an estate planning attorney.

362
00:12:14,289 --> 00:12:17,296
It's also about protecting heirs from themselves.

363
00:12:17,476 --> 00:12:19,841
A twenty year old who suddenly inherits

364
00:12:19,841 --> 00:12:22,025
millions with no guidance is at real

365
00:12:22,025 --> 00:12:22,329
risk.

366
00:12:22,509 --> 00:12:24,996
Structures that release money gradually,

367
00:12:24,996 --> 00:12:27,483
or that require a trustee's approval for

368
00:12:27,483 --> 00:12:30,095
big withdrawals, can give young heirs time

369
00:12:30,095 --> 00:12:32,333
to grow up before they're handed the

370
00:12:32,333 --> 00:12:33,515
keys to everything.

371
00:12:33,865 --> 00:12:36,407
Here's what happens when new money never

372
00:12:36,407 --> 00:12:37,742
learns these lessons.

373
00:12:37,922 --> 00:12:40,627
Cornelius Vanderbilt built one of the largest

374
00:12:40,627 --> 00:12:43,272
fortunes in American history in the eighteen

375
00:12:43,272 --> 00:12:45,736
hundreds, through shipping and railroads.

376
00:12:45,916 --> 00:12:48,031
When he died in eighteen seventy seven,

377
00:12:48,031 --> 00:12:49,874
he was among the richest people in

378
00:12:49,874 --> 00:12:50,525
the country.

379
00:12:50,875 --> 00:12:53,972
His descendants built enormous mansions on Fifth

380
00:12:53,972 --> 00:12:55,391
Avenue and in Newport,

381
00:12:55,391 --> 00:12:57,456
and spent on a scale that's hard

382
00:12:57,456 --> 00:12:58,166
to imagine.

383
00:12:58,346 --> 00:13:00,892
According to the book Fortune's Children,

384
00:13:00,892 --> 00:13:03,065
written by a Vanderbilt descendant,

385
00:13:03,065 --> 00:13:05,363
the fortune was largely gone within a

386
00:13:05,363 --> 00:13:06,357
few generations.

387
00:13:06,537 --> 00:13:08,965
At a family reunion in nineteen seventy

388
00:13:08,965 --> 00:13:10,272
three, the book says,

389
00:13:10,272 --> 00:13:12,701
not one of the gathered descendants was

390
00:13:12,701 --> 00:13:13,572
a millionaire.

391
00:13:13,923 --> 00:13:17,216
Compare that with families like the Rockefellers,

392
00:13:17,216 --> 00:13:18,291
who used trusts,

393
00:13:18,291 --> 00:13:22,189
family offices, philanthropy and strong family governance.

394
00:13:22,369 --> 00:13:24,540
Their wealth has lasted far longer.

395
00:13:24,720 --> 00:13:26,844
The difference wasn't how much money they

396
00:13:26,844 --> 00:13:27,518
started with.

397
00:13:27,698 --> 00:13:29,648
It was the systems they built around

398
00:13:29,648 --> 00:13:29,811
it.

399
00:13:30,161 --> 00:13:31,113
That's angle one.

400
00:13:31,293 --> 00:13:32,708
Assets versus income,

401
00:13:32,708 --> 00:13:34,394
generations versus years,

402
00:13:34,394 --> 00:13:36,753
diversification, how they use debt,

403
00:13:36,753 --> 00:13:39,652
tax planning, spending rate and protection.

404
00:13:39,832 --> 00:13:41,028
Now angle two.

405
00:13:41,208 --> 00:13:42,345
The psychology.

406
00:13:42,525 --> 00:13:45,254
Because the biggest differences between old and

407
00:13:45,254 --> 00:13:46,937
new money are in their heads.

408
00:13:47,288 --> 00:13:49,082
Money changes how people feel,

409
00:13:49,082 --> 00:13:50,936
and how they want others to see

410
00:13:50,936 --> 00:13:51,235
them.

411
00:13:51,415 --> 00:13:53,828
New wealth often comes with something to

412
00:13:53,828 --> 00:13:54,190
prove.

413
00:13:54,370 --> 00:13:56,769
Old wealth often comes with something to

414
00:13:56,769 --> 00:13:57,249
protect.

415
00:13:57,429 --> 00:13:59,895
Those two mindsets lead to very different

416
00:13:59,895 --> 00:14:00,436
behavior.

417
00:14:00,786 --> 00:14:01,970
Difference number eight.

418
00:14:02,150 --> 00:14:04,019
Old money tends to be quiet.

419
00:14:04,199 --> 00:14:05,708
New money is often loud.

420
00:14:06,059 --> 00:14:08,758
Big logos, flashy cars and social media

421
00:14:08,758 --> 00:14:11,597
displays are more common with new wealth.

422
00:14:11,777 --> 00:14:14,586
Old money families often prefer discretion.

423
00:14:14,766 --> 00:14:17,432
Unbranded clothing, older cars,

424
00:14:17,432 --> 00:14:18,981
understated homes.

425
00:14:19,161 --> 00:14:22,336
This style is sometimes called quiet luxury

426
00:14:22,336 --> 00:14:23,665
or stealth wealth.

427
00:14:24,016 --> 00:14:26,732
There are practical reasons for being quiet.

428
00:14:26,912 --> 00:14:29,164
Visible wealth attracts scammers,

429
00:14:29,164 --> 00:14:31,893
lawsuits, and people who want something.

430
00:14:32,073 --> 00:14:34,047
It can also create resentment.

431
00:14:34,227 --> 00:14:36,900
Old money families often teach their children

432
00:14:36,900 --> 00:14:39,277
not to discuss money outside the family.

433
00:14:39,627 --> 00:14:41,020
The lesson for anyone.

434
00:14:41,200 --> 00:14:42,699
You don't need to show what you

435
00:14:42,699 --> 00:14:42,941
have.

436
00:14:43,121 --> 00:14:44,334
Privacy protects you,

437
00:14:44,334 --> 00:14:46,298
and it frees you from the pressure

438
00:14:46,298 --> 00:14:47,626
to keep up appearances.

439
00:14:47,806 --> 00:14:49,594
Let your accounts grow quietly.

440
00:14:49,944 --> 00:14:52,398
There's a famous phrase sometimes used to

441
00:14:52,398 --> 00:14:53,775
describe this attitude.

442
00:14:53,955 --> 00:14:56,277
Money talks, wealth whispers.

443
00:14:56,457 --> 00:14:58,846
Whether or not anyone can prove who

444
00:14:58,846 --> 00:14:59,802
said it first,

445
00:14:59,802 --> 00:15:01,577
it captures the idea well.

446
00:15:01,757 --> 00:15:03,768
Those who have had wealth for a

447
00:15:03,768 --> 00:15:05,843
long time often see no reason to

448
00:15:05,843 --> 00:15:06,621
announce it.

449
00:15:06,801 --> 00:15:09,591
For them, the real luxury is privacy,

450
00:15:09,591 --> 00:15:10,873
time and freedom,

451
00:15:10,873 --> 00:15:13,210
none of which come with a logo.

452
00:15:13,560 --> 00:15:14,953
Difference number nine.

453
00:15:15,133 --> 00:15:16,735
Old money buys quality.

454
00:15:16,915 --> 00:15:18,854
New money often buys brands.

455
00:15:19,204 --> 00:15:21,974
Old money families often value well made

456
00:15:21,974 --> 00:15:23,913
items that last for decades,

457
00:15:23,913 --> 00:15:25,090
even generations.

458
00:15:25,270 --> 00:15:28,367
Furniture, watches, clothing and homes that are

459
00:15:28,367 --> 00:15:30,146
maintained and passed down.

460
00:15:30,326 --> 00:15:32,350
New money is more likely to chase

461
00:15:32,350 --> 00:15:34,680
the newest and most recognized brands.

462
00:15:35,030 --> 00:15:37,672
Buying quality to keep is often cheaper

463
00:15:37,672 --> 00:15:38,350
over time.

464
00:15:38,530 --> 00:15:40,399
A well made item used for thirty

465
00:15:40,399 --> 00:15:42,327
years costs very little per year.

466
00:15:42,507 --> 00:15:45,409
A trendy item replaced every season costs

467
00:15:45,409 --> 00:15:46,117
a fortune.

468
00:15:46,468 --> 00:15:47,768
The lesson for anyone.

469
00:15:47,948 --> 00:15:49,526
For things you use daily,

470
00:15:49,526 --> 00:15:51,547
buy quality and take care of it.

471
00:15:51,727 --> 00:15:52,888
Skip trends.

472
00:15:53,068 --> 00:15:55,533
Ask whether you'd still want this item

473
00:15:55,533 --> 00:15:56,377
in ten years.

474
00:15:56,727 --> 00:15:58,542
You can see this in the idea

475
00:15:58,542 --> 00:15:59,838
of heirloom quality.

476
00:16:00,018 --> 00:16:01,372
A good wool coat,

477
00:16:01,372 --> 00:16:02,886
a solid wood table,

478
00:16:02,886 --> 00:16:05,196
a leather bag that ages well.

479
00:16:05,376 --> 00:16:07,441
These items are often bought once and

480
00:16:07,441 --> 00:16:09,115
repaired rather than replaced.

481
00:16:09,295 --> 00:16:11,451
Over decades, the cost per year can

482
00:16:11,451 --> 00:16:14,100
be lower than buying cheaper versions again

483
00:16:14,100 --> 00:16:14,716
and again.

484
00:16:14,896 --> 00:16:17,490
It's frugality and elegance at the same

485
00:16:17,490 --> 00:16:17,822
time.

486
00:16:18,172 --> 00:16:19,402
Difference number ten.

487
00:16:19,582 --> 00:16:21,254
Old money feels secure.

488
00:16:21,434 --> 00:16:23,341
New money often feels the need to

489
00:16:23,341 --> 00:16:24,093
prove itself.

490
00:16:24,443 --> 00:16:26,011
When you grow up with wealth,

491
00:16:26,011 --> 00:16:27,525
you don't need to show it to

492
00:16:27,525 --> 00:16:28,390
feel successful.

493
00:16:28,570 --> 00:16:29,991
When you've just made it,

494
00:16:29,991 --> 00:16:32,379
especially after growing up without money,

495
00:16:32,379 --> 00:16:34,028
there can be a strong urge to

496
00:16:34,028 --> 00:16:35,733
show the world you've arrived.

497
00:16:36,084 --> 00:16:38,522
That urge is human and understandable.

498
00:16:38,702 --> 00:16:39,921
But it's expensive.

499
00:16:40,101 --> 00:16:42,617
Buying things to prove something to others

500
00:16:42,617 --> 00:16:44,414
is a race with no finish line,

501
00:16:44,414 --> 00:16:46,869
because there's always someone with more.

502
00:16:47,220 --> 00:16:48,543
The lesson for anyone.

503
00:16:48,723 --> 00:16:51,298
Find your security in your balance sheet,

504
00:16:51,298 --> 00:16:53,496
your skills and your relationships,

505
00:16:53,496 --> 00:16:55,318
not in what other people see.

506
00:16:55,668 --> 00:16:58,766
Psychologists have long observed that people often

507
00:16:58,766 --> 00:17:01,617
use possessions to signal identity and status,

508
00:17:01,617 --> 00:17:04,468
especially when they feel insecure about their

509
00:17:04,468 --> 00:17:05,025
position.

510
00:17:05,205 --> 00:17:06,657
That's not a moral failing.

511
00:17:06,837 --> 00:17:07,800
It's very human.

512
00:17:07,980 --> 00:17:09,710
But knowing it gives you a choice.

513
00:17:09,890 --> 00:17:11,643
Next time you feel the urge to

514
00:17:11,643 --> 00:17:13,338
buy something to show others,

515
00:17:13,338 --> 00:17:15,325
ask whether you'd still want it if

516
00:17:15,325 --> 00:17:16,786
nobody would ever see it.

517
00:17:17,137 --> 00:17:18,483
Difference number eleven.

518
00:17:18,663 --> 00:17:19,929
Old money is patient.

519
00:17:20,109 --> 00:17:21,781
New money often moves fast.

520
00:17:22,131 --> 00:17:25,176
Many new fortunes were built with speed,

521
00:17:25,176 --> 00:17:26,927
fast growth, bold bets,

522
00:17:26,927 --> 00:17:28,145
quick decisions.

523
00:17:28,325 --> 00:17:30,159
That works well when you're building.

524
00:17:30,339 --> 00:17:32,683
But when it comes to protecting wealth,

525
00:17:32,683 --> 00:17:34,066
speed can be dangerous.

526
00:17:34,246 --> 00:17:37,313
Quick investments in exciting opportunities are how

527
00:17:37,313 --> 00:17:38,576
many fortunes shrink.

528
00:17:38,927 --> 00:17:40,877
Old money tends to move slowly.

529
00:17:41,057 --> 00:17:42,325
Long due diligence,

530
00:17:42,325 --> 00:17:43,726
long holding periods,

531
00:17:43,726 --> 00:17:45,861
and a willingness to miss out on

532
00:17:45,861 --> 00:17:46,595
hot trends.

533
00:17:46,775 --> 00:17:48,100
They often sound boring,

534
00:17:48,100 --> 00:17:49,260
and that's the point.

535
00:17:49,610 --> 00:17:50,933
The lesson for anyone.

536
00:17:51,113 --> 00:17:53,072
When it comes to your savings,

537
00:17:53,072 --> 00:17:53,725
slow down.

538
00:17:53,905 --> 00:17:55,763
Be suspicious of urgency.

539
00:17:55,943 --> 00:17:58,740
Good investments rarely require you to decide

540
00:17:58,740 --> 00:17:59,113
today.

541
00:17:59,463 --> 00:18:01,878
Old money investors often talk about having

542
00:18:01,878 --> 00:18:02,946
time on their side.

543
00:18:03,126 --> 00:18:04,891
They don't need to make a fortune

544
00:18:04,891 --> 00:18:07,085
this year, because they already have one.

545
00:18:07,265 --> 00:18:09,127
That frees them to wait for good

546
00:18:09,127 --> 00:18:11,222
opportunities, and to say no to most

547
00:18:11,222 --> 00:18:11,688
pitches.

548
00:18:11,868 --> 00:18:13,520
For regular investors,

549
00:18:13,520 --> 00:18:15,247
the same mindset helps.

550
00:18:15,427 --> 00:18:16,886
You don't need to get rich this

551
00:18:16,886 --> 00:18:17,122
year.

552
00:18:17,302 --> 00:18:18,970
You need to stay invested for the

553
00:18:18,970 --> 00:18:19,577
next thirty.

554
00:18:19,927 --> 00:18:20,937
Quick pause.

555
00:18:21,117 --> 00:18:22,537
If you're enjoying this,

556
00:18:22,537 --> 00:18:24,727
please subscribe to Ryan Rover Money.

557
00:18:24,907 --> 00:18:26,831
It's free and it helps us keep

558
00:18:26,831 --> 00:18:29,331
making long, honest videos about money.

559
00:18:29,511 --> 00:18:31,229
Okay, difference twelve.

560
00:18:31,579 --> 00:18:33,065
Difference number twelve.

561
00:18:33,245 --> 00:18:35,822
Old money invests in relationships.

562
00:18:36,172 --> 00:18:40,084
Old money families often maintain long relationships

563
00:18:40,084 --> 00:18:41,815
with advisors, lawyers,

564
00:18:41,815 --> 00:18:43,846
bankers and other families,

565
00:18:43,846 --> 00:18:46,029
sometimes across generations.

566
00:18:46,209 --> 00:18:48,796
These networks provide trusted advice,

567
00:18:48,796 --> 00:18:50,771
opportunities and protection.

568
00:18:51,122 --> 00:18:53,564
New money may move from advisor to

569
00:18:53,564 --> 00:18:56,438
advisor, or rely on whoever pitches them

570
00:18:56,438 --> 00:18:56,869
first.

571
00:18:57,049 --> 00:18:58,839
That can lead to bad advice and

572
00:18:58,839 --> 00:18:59,417
high fees.

573
00:18:59,597 --> 00:19:01,218
Trust takes time to build,

574
00:19:01,218 --> 00:19:02,964
and it compounds like money.

575
00:19:03,314 --> 00:19:04,684
The lesson for anyone.

576
00:19:04,864 --> 00:19:08,647
Build long term relationships with trustworthy professionals

577
00:19:08,647 --> 00:19:09,403
and mentors.

578
00:19:09,583 --> 00:19:10,846
Look for fiduciaries,

579
00:19:10,846 --> 00:19:13,130
people legally required to act in your

580
00:19:13,130 --> 00:19:13,972
best interest.

581
00:19:14,322 --> 00:19:15,703
For ordinary people,

582
00:19:15,703 --> 00:19:17,913
this can be as simple as finding

583
00:19:17,913 --> 00:19:19,640
one trusted tax preparer,

584
00:19:19,640 --> 00:19:21,367
one good insurance agent,

585
00:19:21,367 --> 00:19:24,337
and perhaps one fee only financial planner,

586
00:19:24,337 --> 00:19:26,617
and sticking with them for years.

587
00:19:26,797 --> 00:19:28,869
Over time, they learn your situation,

588
00:19:28,869 --> 00:19:30,829
and you learn whether you can trust

589
00:19:30,829 --> 00:19:31,557
their advice.

590
00:19:31,737 --> 00:19:34,123
That kind of relationship is worth more

591
00:19:34,123 --> 00:19:35,347
than any single tip.

592
00:19:35,698 --> 00:19:37,242
Difference number thirteen.

593
00:19:37,422 --> 00:19:40,495
Old money families often build identity around

594
00:19:40,495 --> 00:19:41,764
values, not wealth.

595
00:19:42,114 --> 00:19:45,290
Families that keep wealth for generations often

596
00:19:45,290 --> 00:19:46,912
emphasize shared values,

597
00:19:46,912 --> 00:19:48,534
like education, service,

598
00:19:48,534 --> 00:19:50,426
work ethic and philanthropy.

599
00:19:50,606 --> 00:19:52,477
Money is seen as a tool to

600
00:19:52,477 --> 00:19:53,988
support those values,

601
00:19:53,988 --> 00:19:55,715
not the identity itself.

602
00:19:56,065 --> 00:19:58,359
When identity is tied only to money,

603
00:19:58,359 --> 00:20:01,227
people can make reckless decisions to protect

604
00:20:01,227 --> 00:20:02,055
their status.

605
00:20:02,235 --> 00:20:04,353
When identity is tied to values,

606
00:20:04,353 --> 00:20:06,403
money decisions become clearer.

607
00:20:06,754 --> 00:20:08,112
The lesson for anyone.

608
00:20:08,292 --> 00:20:10,533
Write down what your family stands for.

609
00:20:10,713 --> 00:20:13,442
Let those values guide spending and saving

610
00:20:13,442 --> 00:20:14,091
decisions.

611
00:20:14,442 --> 00:20:16,709
This is also why old money families

612
00:20:16,709 --> 00:20:18,782
often encourage meaningful work,

613
00:20:18,782 --> 00:20:20,919
even for heirs who don't need the

614
00:20:20,919 --> 00:20:21,373
income.

615
00:20:21,553 --> 00:20:22,984
Work gives purpose,

616
00:20:22,984 --> 00:20:24,490
identity and skills.

617
00:20:24,670 --> 00:20:26,687
Heirs who find meaningful roles,

618
00:20:26,687 --> 00:20:28,011
in a family business,

619
00:20:28,011 --> 00:20:29,586
a career or philanthropy,

620
00:20:29,586 --> 00:20:31,918
are often far more likely to preserve

621
00:20:31,918 --> 00:20:34,817
wealth than those who drift without direction.

622
00:20:35,168 --> 00:20:36,735
Difference number fourteen.

623
00:20:36,915 --> 00:20:38,924
New money takes big risks.

624
00:20:39,104 --> 00:20:40,845
Old money manages risk.

625
00:20:41,195 --> 00:20:43,027
This is where new money has a

626
00:20:43,027 --> 00:20:43,911
real strength.

627
00:20:44,091 --> 00:20:47,656
Building a fortune usually requires taking risks.

628
00:20:47,836 --> 00:20:49,132
Starting a company,

629
00:20:49,132 --> 00:20:51,725
changing careers, betting on yourself.

630
00:20:51,905 --> 00:20:53,337
Many old money heirs,

631
00:20:53,337 --> 00:20:55,043
raised to protect wealth,

632
00:20:55,043 --> 00:20:57,907
become too cautious to build anything new.

633
00:20:58,258 --> 00:21:00,429
The best approach often combines both.

634
00:21:00,609 --> 00:21:02,787
Take smart risks with your career and

635
00:21:02,787 --> 00:21:04,377
a small part of your money,

636
00:21:04,377 --> 00:21:06,733
while protecting the core of your wealth

637
00:21:06,733 --> 00:21:08,794
with diversification and insurance.

638
00:21:09,144 --> 00:21:10,525
The lesson for anyone.

639
00:21:10,705 --> 00:21:12,807
Separate your money into two buckets.

640
00:21:12,987 --> 00:21:14,135
A protected core,

641
00:21:14,135 --> 00:21:16,769
and a small amount for calculated risks

642
00:21:16,769 --> 00:21:17,782
you understand.

643
00:21:18,132 --> 00:21:20,439
Many self made fortunes were built by

644
00:21:20,439 --> 00:21:22,870
people who put almost everything on one

645
00:21:22,870 --> 00:21:23,182
idea.

646
00:21:23,362 --> 00:21:25,209
But once the fortune exists,

647
00:21:25,209 --> 00:21:28,311
the smartest builders often change strategy and

648
00:21:28,311 --> 00:21:30,026
start acting like keepers.

649
00:21:30,206 --> 00:21:33,143
They sell part of their concentrated position,

650
00:21:33,143 --> 00:21:35,442
diversify, and protect the downside.

651
00:21:35,622 --> 00:21:38,516
Building and keeping really are two different

652
00:21:38,516 --> 00:21:38,966
skills.

653
00:21:39,316 --> 00:21:40,326
That's angle two.

654
00:21:40,506 --> 00:21:41,897
Quiet versus loud,

655
00:21:41,897 --> 00:21:43,598
quality versus brands,

656
00:21:43,598 --> 00:21:45,453
security versus proving,

657
00:21:45,453 --> 00:21:47,153
patience versus speed,

658
00:21:47,153 --> 00:21:49,318
networks, identity and risk.

659
00:21:49,498 --> 00:21:50,705
Now angle three.

660
00:21:50,885 --> 00:21:51,663
The playbook.

661
00:21:51,843 --> 00:21:53,326
How old money keeps it,

662
00:21:53,326 --> 00:21:55,326
and what new money does better.

663
00:21:55,676 --> 00:21:58,648
Keeping wealth for generations doesn't happen by

664
00:21:58,648 --> 00:21:59,205
accident.

665
00:21:59,385 --> 00:22:00,813
It takes systems.

666
00:22:00,993 --> 00:22:03,244
The families that last often share a

667
00:22:03,244 --> 00:22:04,557
handful of practices.

668
00:22:04,737 --> 00:22:07,100
And some of them are surprisingly easy

669
00:22:07,100 --> 00:22:09,028
to copy, even for a family with

670
00:22:09,028 --> 00:22:10,148
no fortune at all.

671
00:22:10,498 --> 00:22:12,088
Difference number fifteen.

672
00:22:12,268 --> 00:22:14,983
Old money families hold family meetings about

673
00:22:14,983 --> 00:22:15,345
money.

674
00:22:15,695 --> 00:22:18,939
Many wealthy families hold regular meetings to

675
00:22:18,939 --> 00:22:20,913
discuss investments, values,

676
00:22:20,913 --> 00:22:23,311
philanthropy and responsibilities.

677
00:22:23,491 --> 00:22:26,068
Some have written family constitutions.

678
00:22:26,248 --> 00:22:29,342
The Williams Group research suggested that breakdowns

679
00:22:29,342 --> 00:22:32,086
in trust and communication within families were

680
00:22:32,086 --> 00:22:34,538
among the biggest reasons wealth was lost.

681
00:22:34,888 --> 00:22:37,215
New money often keeps money secret,

682
00:22:37,215 --> 00:22:39,276
even from spouses and children.

683
00:22:39,456 --> 00:22:41,585
Then, when wealth is passed on,

684
00:22:41,585 --> 00:22:44,332
heirs are unprepared and families fight.

685
00:22:44,683 --> 00:22:46,087
The lesson for anyone.

686
00:22:46,267 --> 00:22:48,795
Hold a simple family money meeting once

687
00:22:48,795 --> 00:22:49,832
or twice a year.

688
00:22:50,012 --> 00:22:51,719
Share goals and values,

689
00:22:51,719 --> 00:22:53,797
and age appropriate details.

690
00:22:54,147 --> 00:22:56,314
A family meeting doesn't need to be

691
00:22:56,314 --> 00:22:56,747
formal.

692
00:22:56,927 --> 00:22:58,814
It can be a dinner where you

693
00:22:58,814 --> 00:22:59,824
share one goal,

694
00:22:59,824 --> 00:23:01,711
one decision and one lesson.

695
00:23:01,891 --> 00:23:04,218
Older kids can learn how the household

696
00:23:04,218 --> 00:23:05,014
budget works.

697
00:23:05,194 --> 00:23:08,391
Adult children can learn where important documents

698
00:23:08,391 --> 00:23:08,967
are kept.

699
00:23:09,147 --> 00:23:12,860
Those simple conversations prevent confusion and conflict

700
00:23:12,860 --> 00:23:15,530
later, which is exactly where many family

701
00:23:15,530 --> 00:23:16,833
fortunes break down.

702
00:23:17,183 --> 00:23:18,704
Difference number sixteen.

703
00:23:18,884 --> 00:23:21,431
Old money prepares heirs before giving them

704
00:23:21,431 --> 00:23:21,786
money.

705
00:23:22,136 --> 00:23:25,193
The same research found that unprepared heirs

706
00:23:25,193 --> 00:23:27,639
were a major cause of lost fortunes.

707
00:23:27,819 --> 00:23:30,748
Old money families often teach children about

708
00:23:30,748 --> 00:23:34,003
money early, give them responsibilities gradually,

709
00:23:34,003 --> 00:23:36,933
and sometimes release inheritances in stages.

710
00:23:37,283 --> 00:23:39,919
Thomas Stanley also found that large ongoing

711
00:23:39,919 --> 00:23:42,317
gifts to adult children were linked with

712
00:23:42,317 --> 00:23:45,193
those children accumulating less wealth of their

713
00:23:45,193 --> 00:23:45,433
own.

714
00:23:45,613 --> 00:23:47,854
Preparation matters more than the amount.

715
00:23:48,204 --> 00:23:49,585
The lesson for anyone.

716
00:23:49,765 --> 00:23:51,977
Teach your kids about money early,

717
00:23:51,977 --> 00:23:52,888
let them earn,

718
00:23:52,888 --> 00:23:54,774
let them make small mistakes,

719
00:23:54,774 --> 00:23:57,962
and give responsibility before giving large sums.

720
00:23:58,312 --> 00:24:01,053
Some families use what are sometimes called

721
00:24:01,053 --> 00:24:04,113
incentive trusts, which release money when heirs

722
00:24:04,113 --> 00:24:05,707
reach certain milestones,

723
00:24:05,707 --> 00:24:08,449
like finishing school or matching their own

724
00:24:08,449 --> 00:24:09,341
earned income.

725
00:24:09,521 --> 00:24:11,445
Whether or not you ever set up

726
00:24:11,445 --> 00:24:13,689
a trust, the principle is valuable.

727
00:24:13,869 --> 00:24:16,276
Money works best when it supports effort

728
00:24:16,276 --> 00:24:19,105
and responsibility, rather than replacing them.

729
00:24:19,455 --> 00:24:21,022
Difference number seventeen.

730
00:24:21,202 --> 00:24:23,164
Old money gives as a family.

731
00:24:23,514 --> 00:24:26,344
Many old money families run foundations or

732
00:24:26,344 --> 00:24:27,287
give together.

733
00:24:27,467 --> 00:24:31,341
Philanthropy can unite family members around shared

734
00:24:31,341 --> 00:24:35,520
values, teach younger generations about responsibility,

735
00:24:35,520 --> 00:24:36,887
and, under US law,

736
00:24:36,887 --> 00:24:38,787
offer legal tax benefits.

737
00:24:39,137 --> 00:24:41,517
New money is increasingly generous too.

738
00:24:41,697 --> 00:24:44,283
Many self made billionaires have pledged to

739
00:24:44,283 --> 00:24:46,689
give away large parts of their fortunes.

740
00:24:46,869 --> 00:24:48,971
The habit of giving keeps money in

741
00:24:48,971 --> 00:24:49,713
perspective.

742
00:24:50,064 --> 00:24:51,422
The lesson for anyone.

743
00:24:51,602 --> 00:24:53,811
Choose a cause as a family and

744
00:24:53,811 --> 00:24:56,316
give together, even small amounts.

745
00:24:56,496 --> 00:24:58,841
It builds values that outlast money.

746
00:24:59,191 --> 00:25:02,268
Andrew Carnegie, one of the richest Americans

747
00:25:02,268 --> 00:25:03,020
of his era,

748
00:25:03,020 --> 00:25:05,550
famously argued that the man who dies

749
00:25:05,550 --> 00:25:06,917
rich dies disgraced,

750
00:25:06,917 --> 00:25:09,174
and gave away most of his fortune

751
00:25:09,174 --> 00:25:12,182
to libraries, universities and other causes.

752
00:25:12,362 --> 00:25:14,432
More recently, the Giving Pledge,

753
00:25:14,432 --> 00:25:16,816
started by Warren Buffett and Bill and

754
00:25:16,816 --> 00:25:18,134
Melinda French Gates,

755
00:25:18,134 --> 00:25:20,957
invites billionaires to commit to giving away

756
00:25:20,957 --> 00:25:22,776
the majority of their wealth.

757
00:25:23,127 --> 00:25:24,694
Difference number eighteen.

758
00:25:24,874 --> 00:25:26,743
Old money holds for decades.

759
00:25:27,093 --> 00:25:29,536
Old money families often keep land,

760
00:25:29,536 --> 00:25:32,538
businesses and investments for generations.

761
00:25:32,718 --> 00:25:35,144
Long holding periods reduce taxes,

762
00:25:35,144 --> 00:25:37,927
trading costs and the risk of emotional

763
00:25:37,927 --> 00:25:38,569
mistakes.

764
00:25:38,749 --> 00:25:40,630
They let compounding do its work.

765
00:25:40,980 --> 00:25:44,245
New money investors may trade more frequently,

766
00:25:44,245 --> 00:25:45,310
chasing trends.

767
00:25:45,490 --> 00:25:47,786
Research on individual investors,

768
00:25:47,786 --> 00:25:50,570
like studies by Brad Barber and Terrance

769
00:25:50,570 --> 00:25:53,701
Odean, has found that frequent traders tended

770
00:25:53,701 --> 00:25:56,067
to earn lower returns after costs.

771
00:25:56,417 --> 00:25:57,775
The lesson for anyone.

772
00:25:57,955 --> 00:26:00,762
Buy diversified investments and hold them for

773
00:26:00,762 --> 00:26:01,635
the long term.

774
00:26:01,815 --> 00:26:03,373
Check them occasionally,

775
00:26:03,373 --> 00:26:04,346
not constantly.

776
00:26:04,697 --> 00:26:07,003
The research by Barber and Odean looked

777
00:26:07,003 --> 00:26:09,547
at tens of thousands of household brokerage

778
00:26:09,547 --> 00:26:11,558
accounts in the nineteen nineties.

779
00:26:11,738 --> 00:26:14,210
The households that traded the most earned

780
00:26:14,210 --> 00:26:16,624
noticeably lower returns than the market,

781
00:26:16,624 --> 00:26:19,155
largely because of trading costs and poorly

782
00:26:19,155 --> 00:26:20,097
timed decisions.

783
00:26:20,277 --> 00:26:22,727
Doing less was often the winning strategy.

784
00:26:23,077 --> 00:26:24,818
Difference number nineteen.

785
00:26:24,998 --> 00:26:26,426
What new money does better.

786
00:26:26,776 --> 00:26:28,981
New money has real strengths.

787
00:26:29,161 --> 00:26:32,812
Hunger, creativity, risk taking and a willingness

788
00:26:32,812 --> 00:26:34,525
to work extremely hard.

789
00:26:34,705 --> 00:26:37,465
Ramsey Solutions' study found that most American

790
00:26:37,465 --> 00:26:39,477
millionaires were first generation.

791
00:26:39,657 --> 00:26:41,871
And UBS has reported that self made

792
00:26:41,871 --> 00:26:44,464
entrepreneurs create a large share of new

793
00:26:44,464 --> 00:26:46,298
billionaire wealth each year.

794
00:26:46,648 --> 00:26:49,306
Old money heirs sometimes lose that drive.

795
00:26:49,486 --> 00:26:51,232
Without the need to build,

796
00:26:51,232 --> 00:26:51,971
some drift.

797
00:26:52,151 --> 00:26:54,148
That's one reason fortunes fade.

798
00:26:54,328 --> 00:26:56,794
The families that last often find ways

799
00:26:56,794 --> 00:27:00,040
to keep entrepreneurship alive in each generation.

800
00:27:00,390 --> 00:27:01,794
The lesson for anyone.

801
00:27:01,974 --> 00:27:03,820
Keep the hustle of new money.

802
00:27:04,000 --> 00:27:07,150
Build skills, take smart risks and create

803
00:27:07,150 --> 00:27:07,611
value.

804
00:27:07,791 --> 00:27:10,119
Then add the protection habits of old

805
00:27:10,119 --> 00:27:10,496
money.

806
00:27:10,847 --> 00:27:12,984
Think of the immigrant shop owner,

807
00:27:12,984 --> 00:27:16,002
the first generation college graduate who starts

808
00:27:16,002 --> 00:27:18,580
a company, or the tradesperson who builds

809
00:27:18,580 --> 00:27:21,284
a contracting business from a single truck.

810
00:27:21,464 --> 00:27:24,482
Their drive, frugality and appetite for work

811
00:27:24,482 --> 00:27:27,156
are exactly what old money families try

812
00:27:27,156 --> 00:27:29,557
to keep alive in later generations,

813
00:27:29,557 --> 00:27:31,065
often without success.

814
00:27:31,416 --> 00:27:32,960
And difference number twenty.

815
00:27:33,140 --> 00:27:34,986
The best approach combines both.

816
00:27:35,336 --> 00:27:36,415
Build like new money.

817
00:27:36,595 --> 00:27:38,567
Work hard, take smart risks,

818
00:27:38,567 --> 00:27:40,961
grow your income and create value.

819
00:27:41,141 --> 00:27:42,232
Keep like old money.

820
00:27:42,412 --> 00:27:44,045
Spend below your means,

821
00:27:44,045 --> 00:27:45,892
diversify, plan for taxes,

822
00:27:45,892 --> 00:27:47,525
protect with structure,

823
00:27:47,525 --> 00:27:48,661
think long term,

824
00:27:48,661 --> 00:27:50,934
and prepare the next generation.

825
00:27:51,284 --> 00:27:54,380
That combination is exactly what many self

826
00:27:54,380 --> 00:27:55,928
made millionaires do.

827
00:27:56,108 --> 00:27:57,330
They build with hunger,

828
00:27:57,330 --> 00:27:58,871
then protect with discipline.

829
00:27:59,051 --> 00:28:01,235
And some of them become the founders

830
00:28:01,235 --> 00:28:02,812
of new old money families.

831
00:28:03,163 --> 00:28:04,556
The lesson for anyone.

832
00:28:04,736 --> 00:28:07,000
Ask yourself which side you're stronger on.

833
00:28:07,180 --> 00:28:08,689
Building or keeping.

834
00:28:08,869 --> 00:28:10,576
Then work on the other side.

835
00:28:10,926 --> 00:28:13,846
Picture someone who builds a small business

836
00:28:13,846 --> 00:28:16,019
with long hours and smart risks,

837
00:28:16,019 --> 00:28:18,532
then pays themselves a modest salary,

838
00:28:18,532 --> 00:28:21,724
invests the profits in diversified index funds,

839
00:28:21,724 --> 00:28:22,675
writes a will,

840
00:28:22,675 --> 00:28:24,508
teaches their kids to work,

841
00:28:24,508 --> 00:28:27,225
and talks openly with their family about

842
00:28:27,225 --> 00:28:27,632
money.

843
00:28:27,812 --> 00:28:30,359
That person is building the first chapter

844
00:28:30,359 --> 00:28:31,725
of an old money story.

845
00:28:32,075 --> 00:28:34,765
Let's name the classic mistakes that sink

846
00:28:34,765 --> 00:28:37,061
new fortunes, so you can avoid them

847
00:28:37,061 --> 00:28:37,914
at any level.

848
00:28:38,094 --> 00:28:38,930
Mistake one.

849
00:28:39,110 --> 00:28:41,026
Upgrading everything at once.

850
00:28:41,206 --> 00:28:43,740
A sudden jump in lifestyle locks in

851
00:28:43,740 --> 00:28:46,130
high fixed costs that are hard to

852
00:28:46,130 --> 00:28:46,709
reverse.

853
00:28:46,889 --> 00:28:48,062
Mistake two.

854
00:28:48,242 --> 00:28:51,102
Lending or giving money to everyone who

855
00:28:51,102 --> 00:28:52,642
asks, without limits.

856
00:28:52,822 --> 00:28:54,953
It can drain a fortune and damage

857
00:28:54,953 --> 00:28:56,955
relationships at the same time.

858
00:28:57,305 --> 00:28:58,257
Mistake three.

859
00:28:58,437 --> 00:29:01,677
Investing in friends' businesses or exciting deals

860
00:29:01,677 --> 00:29:03,557
without proper due diligence.

861
00:29:03,737 --> 00:29:04,816
Mistake four.

862
00:29:04,996 --> 00:29:07,899
Ignoring taxes until the bill arrives.

863
00:29:08,079 --> 00:29:09,367
And mistake five.

864
00:29:09,547 --> 00:29:12,137
Assuming the income will continue forever.

865
00:29:12,317 --> 00:29:14,613
Careers end, markets change,

866
00:29:14,613 --> 00:29:16,171
and companies fail.

867
00:29:16,351 --> 00:29:18,331
Old money families plan as if the

868
00:29:18,331 --> 00:29:19,890
good times might not last,

869
00:29:19,890 --> 00:29:22,110
which is exactly why theirs often do.

870
00:29:22,460 --> 00:29:24,864
The antidote to all five is a

871
00:29:24,864 --> 00:29:25,362
pause.

872
00:29:25,542 --> 00:29:27,002
When new money arrives,

873
00:29:27,002 --> 00:29:28,588
whether it's a big raise,

874
00:29:28,588 --> 00:29:30,239
a bonus or an inheritance,

875
00:29:30,239 --> 00:29:33,222
many planners suggest parking it somewhere safe

876
00:29:33,222 --> 00:29:35,380
for a few months before making big

877
00:29:35,380 --> 00:29:36,014
decisions.

878
00:29:36,194 --> 00:29:39,075
Time cools emotions and lets a clear

879
00:29:39,075 --> 00:29:39,875
plan form.

880
00:29:40,055 --> 00:29:41,830
The money will still be there in

881
00:29:41,830 --> 00:29:42,551
three months.

882
00:29:42,731 --> 00:29:43,957
A rushed decision,

883
00:29:43,957 --> 00:29:45,184
on the other hand,

884
00:29:45,184 --> 00:29:46,887
can be very hard to undo.

885
00:29:47,237 --> 00:29:48,548
Why does this matter now?

886
00:29:48,728 --> 00:29:50,825
Because America is in the middle of

887
00:29:50,825 --> 00:29:53,581
what's often called the great wealth transfer.

888
00:29:53,761 --> 00:29:57,106
Research firms like Cerulli Associates have estimated

889
00:29:57,106 --> 00:29:59,504
that tens of trillions of dollars will

890
00:29:59,504 --> 00:30:01,902
pass from older Americans to heirs and

891
00:30:01,902 --> 00:30:04,048
charities over the coming decades.

892
00:30:04,228 --> 00:30:07,110
UBS has also reported that inherited wealth

893
00:30:07,110 --> 00:30:09,254
is creating a large share of new

894
00:30:09,254 --> 00:30:10,126
billionaires.

895
00:30:10,476 --> 00:30:12,021
If the old pattern holds,

896
00:30:12,021 --> 00:30:13,938
much of that money will be gone

897
00:30:13,938 --> 00:30:15,607
within a generation or two.

898
00:30:15,787 --> 00:30:17,503
The families who keep it will be

899
00:30:17,503 --> 00:30:19,166
the ones with old money habits.

900
00:30:19,346 --> 00:30:22,634
Communication, preparation, diversification,

901
00:30:22,634 --> 00:30:25,847
spending discipline and long term thinking.

902
00:30:26,198 --> 00:30:28,766
And for families without big inheritances,

903
00:30:28,766 --> 00:30:31,213
the same habits can start something new.

904
00:30:31,393 --> 00:30:34,025
Every old money family began with someone

905
00:30:34,025 --> 00:30:35,886
who built and then protected.

906
00:30:36,066 --> 00:30:37,274
That person could be you.

907
00:30:37,624 --> 00:30:39,883
So how would an ordinary family start

908
00:30:39,883 --> 00:30:41,044
becoming old money,

909
00:30:41,044 --> 00:30:41,838
from scratch?

910
00:30:42,018 --> 00:30:44,026
Here's a simple thirty year outline.

911
00:30:44,377 --> 00:30:45,642
Years one to ten.

912
00:30:45,822 --> 00:30:46,286
Build.

913
00:30:46,466 --> 00:30:47,684
Grow your income,

914
00:30:47,684 --> 00:30:48,686
live below it,

915
00:30:48,686 --> 00:30:52,625
and invest the difference automatically in diversified,

916
00:30:52,625 --> 00:30:53,699
low cost funds.

917
00:30:53,879 --> 00:30:55,575
Pay off high interest debt.

918
00:30:55,755 --> 00:30:57,333
Build an emergency fund.

919
00:30:57,513 --> 00:30:59,859
Write a will and name beneficiaries.

920
00:31:00,039 --> 00:31:01,602
Teach your kids the basics,

921
00:31:01,602 --> 00:31:04,092
like saving, earning and waiting for things

922
00:31:04,092 --> 00:31:04,671
they want.

923
00:31:05,021 --> 00:31:06,321
Years eleven to twenty.

924
00:31:06,501 --> 00:31:08,231
Grow and protect.

925
00:31:08,411 --> 00:31:11,135
Keep raising your savings rate with every

926
00:31:11,135 --> 00:31:11,534
raise.

927
00:31:11,714 --> 00:31:13,718
Add income producing assets,

928
00:31:13,718 --> 00:31:16,153
maybe a rental property or a small

929
00:31:16,153 --> 00:31:18,587
business, if they fit your skills.

930
00:31:18,767 --> 00:31:21,275
Review insurance and estate plans.

931
00:31:21,455 --> 00:31:23,100
Start family money meetings,

932
00:31:23,100 --> 00:31:25,627
and give older kids real responsibility for

933
00:31:25,627 --> 00:31:27,213
part of their own spending.

934
00:31:27,564 --> 00:31:28,910
Years twenty one to thirty.

935
00:31:29,090 --> 00:31:30,634
Keep and pass on.

936
00:31:30,814 --> 00:31:32,804
Shift toward preserving wealth,

937
00:31:32,804 --> 00:31:36,333
with careful diversification and a sustainable spending

938
00:31:36,333 --> 00:31:36,654
rate.

939
00:31:36,834 --> 00:31:39,839
Involve adult children in family decisions and

940
00:31:39,839 --> 00:31:40,689
philanthropy.

941
00:31:40,869 --> 00:31:43,446
Prepare them before passing anything on.

942
00:31:43,626 --> 00:31:45,858
By then, you've done what every old

943
00:31:45,858 --> 00:31:47,260
money family once did.

944
00:31:47,440 --> 00:31:48,555
You built something,

945
00:31:48,555 --> 00:31:50,617
and taught the next generation how to

946
00:31:50,617 --> 00:31:51,062
keep it.

947
00:31:51,413 --> 00:31:52,611
From old money,

948
00:31:52,611 --> 00:31:53,491
copy these.

949
00:31:53,671 --> 00:31:55,087
Live on less than you earn.

950
00:31:55,267 --> 00:31:57,427
Build income producing assets.

951
00:31:57,607 --> 00:31:58,570
Diversify.

952
00:31:58,750 --> 00:32:00,051
Think in decades.

953
00:32:00,231 --> 00:32:02,483
Have a will and name beneficiaries.

954
00:32:02,663 --> 00:32:04,161
Teach your kids about money.

955
00:32:04,341 --> 00:32:05,815
And keep your wealth quiet.

956
00:32:06,165 --> 00:32:07,283
From new money,

957
00:32:07,283 --> 00:32:08,103
copy these.

958
00:32:08,283 --> 00:32:09,340
Grow your income.

959
00:32:09,520 --> 00:32:11,854
Take calculated risks with your career.

960
00:32:12,034 --> 00:32:14,100
Build skills that create value.

961
00:32:14,280 --> 00:32:15,871
Be willing to start something.

962
00:32:16,051 --> 00:32:17,107
And stay hungry.

963
00:32:17,287 --> 00:32:20,643
Together, those habits are a powerful combination

964
00:32:20,643 --> 00:32:22,012
at any income level.

965
00:32:22,363 --> 00:32:23,257
Question one.

966
00:32:23,437 --> 00:32:25,491
Is old money always better with money?

967
00:32:25,671 --> 00:32:25,904
No.

968
00:32:26,084 --> 00:32:28,478
Plenty of old money families lose their

969
00:32:28,478 --> 00:32:30,933
wealth, and plenty of new money builders

970
00:32:30,933 --> 00:32:32,283
manage it brilliantly.

971
00:32:32,463 --> 00:32:33,688
These are patterns,

972
00:32:33,688 --> 00:32:34,333
not rules.

973
00:32:34,513 --> 00:32:36,417
The habits matter more than the label.

974
00:32:36,767 --> 00:32:37,695
Question two.

975
00:32:37,875 --> 00:32:39,275
Do I need to be rich to

976
00:32:39,275 --> 00:32:41,161
use trusts and estate planning?

977
00:32:41,341 --> 00:32:42,572
Not necessarily.

978
00:32:42,752 --> 00:32:45,845
A basic will and beneficiary designations are

979
00:32:45,845 --> 00:32:47,906
important for almost everyone.

980
00:32:48,086 --> 00:32:50,823
Trusts can be useful for families with

981
00:32:50,823 --> 00:32:53,991
young children, property in multiple states,

982
00:32:53,991 --> 00:32:55,575
or complex situations.

983
00:32:55,755 --> 00:32:58,097
An estate planning attorney can help you

984
00:32:58,097 --> 00:32:58,506
decide.

985
00:32:58,857 --> 00:32:59,809
Question three.

986
00:32:59,989 --> 00:33:02,579
What's the single most important old money

987
00:33:02,579 --> 00:33:02,949
habit?

988
00:33:03,129 --> 00:33:05,493
Probably spending only part of what your

989
00:33:05,493 --> 00:33:07,680
assets earn, and never the principal.

990
00:33:07,860 --> 00:33:08,971
That one rule,

991
00:33:08,971 --> 00:33:10,716
followed over decades,

992
00:33:10,716 --> 00:33:12,620
is how fortunes survive.

993
00:33:12,971 --> 00:33:13,853
Question four.

994
00:33:14,033 --> 00:33:16,209
Is quiet luxury just another trend to

995
00:33:16,209 --> 00:33:16,738
buy into?

996
00:33:16,918 --> 00:33:17,870
It can be.

997
00:33:18,050 --> 00:33:21,162
Some brands now sell expensive plain clothing

998
00:33:21,162 --> 00:33:22,822
labeled as quiet luxury.

999
00:33:23,002 --> 00:33:24,105
That misses the point.

1000
00:33:24,285 --> 00:33:26,009
The old money idea isn't buying a

1001
00:33:26,009 --> 00:33:26,792
different look.

1002
00:33:26,972 --> 00:33:29,328
It's caring less about looks altogether,

1003
00:33:29,328 --> 00:33:31,802
and putting the money into assets instead.

1004
00:33:32,153 --> 00:33:32,954
Quick recap.

1005
00:33:33,134 --> 00:33:34,991
Angle one, the numbers.

1006
00:33:35,171 --> 00:33:36,865
Assets versus income,

1007
00:33:36,865 --> 00:33:38,882
generations versus years,

1008
00:33:38,882 --> 00:33:41,464
diversified versus concentrated,

1009
00:33:41,464 --> 00:33:42,674
debt as a tool,

1010
00:33:42,674 --> 00:33:44,933
tax planning, spending rate,

1011
00:33:44,933 --> 00:33:46,143
and protection.

1012
00:33:46,493 --> 00:33:48,524
Angle two, the psychology.

1013
00:33:48,704 --> 00:33:50,060
Quiet versus loud,

1014
00:33:50,060 --> 00:33:51,716
quality versus brands,

1015
00:33:51,716 --> 00:33:53,523
security versus proving,

1016
00:33:53,523 --> 00:33:55,180
patience versus speed,

1017
00:33:55,180 --> 00:33:57,589
networks, identity beyond money,

1018
00:33:57,589 --> 00:33:58,944
and risk appetite.

1019
00:33:59,295 --> 00:34:00,955
Angle three, the playbook.

1020
00:34:01,135 --> 00:34:03,461
Family meetings, preparing heirs,

1021
00:34:03,461 --> 00:34:05,716
philanthropy, long term holding,

1022
00:34:05,716 --> 00:34:07,619
what new money does better,

1023
00:34:07,619 --> 00:34:09,099
and the best of both.

1024
00:34:09,450 --> 00:34:11,573
Here's what I find most encouraging about

1025
00:34:11,573 --> 00:34:12,143
this topic.

1026
00:34:12,323 --> 00:34:13,786
Every old money family,

1027
00:34:13,786 --> 00:34:16,711
without exception, started with one person who

1028
00:34:16,711 --> 00:34:19,382
built something and decided to protect it.

1029
00:34:19,562 --> 00:34:21,096
Their habits weren't secret,

1030
00:34:21,096 --> 00:34:22,685
and they weren't complicated.

1031
00:34:22,865 --> 00:34:24,635
Spend less than you earn,

1032
00:34:24,635 --> 00:34:26,616
own assets, think long term,

1033
00:34:26,616 --> 00:34:28,740
and teach the next generation.

1034
00:34:28,920 --> 00:34:31,007
You don't need a famous last name

1035
00:34:31,007 --> 00:34:32,526
to think like old money,

1036
00:34:32,526 --> 00:34:34,487
or a startup exit to build like

1037
00:34:34,487 --> 00:34:35,119
new money.

1038
00:34:35,299 --> 00:34:36,386
Build like new,

1039
00:34:36,386 --> 00:34:37,401
keep like old.

1040
00:34:37,581 --> 00:34:39,015
If you want the full plan,

1041
00:34:39,015 --> 00:34:40,118
it's in my playbook,

1042
00:34:40,118 --> 00:34:41,551
linked in the description.

1043
00:34:41,731 --> 00:34:43,166
Tell me in the comments,

1044
00:34:43,166 --> 00:34:44,839
are you more of a builder or

1045
00:34:44,839 --> 00:34:45,377
a keeper?

1046
00:34:45,557 --> 00:34:46,904
I'm Ryan Rover.

1047
00:34:47,084 --> 00:34:48,198
See you in the next one.
